            %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/pspum-83/"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/PSPM83-cover.png", :width => "300"}/
          %p
            %strong Edited collection:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Mathematical+Foundations+of+Quantum+Field+and+Perturbative+String+Theory"} Mathematical Foundations of
                %br/
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Mathematical+Foundations+of+Quantum+Field+and+Perturbative+String+Theory"} Quantum Field Theory and Perturbative String Theory
              %p
                (with
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hisham+Sati"} H. Sati
              %p
                Proceedings of Symposia in Pure Mathematics
                %strong 83
              %p American Mathematical Society (2011)
              %p
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/pspum-83"}> ams:pspum-83
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %blockquote
            %p
              %strong Cover blurb:
              Conceptual progress in fundamental
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theoretical+physics"} theoretical physics
              is linked with the search for the suitable
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+structures"} mathematical structures
              that model the physical systems.
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} Quantum field theory
              (QFT) has proven to be a rich source of ideas for mathematics for a long time. However, fundamental questions such as “What is a QFT?” did not have satisfactory mathematical answers, especially on spaces with arbitrary topology, fundamental for the formulation of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative string theory
            %p This book contains a collection of papers highlighting the mathematical foundations of QFT and its relevance to perturbative string theory as well as the deep techniques that have been emerging in the last few years.
            %p The papers are organized under three main chapters: Foundations for Quantum Field Theory, Quantization of Field Theories, and Two-Dimensional Quantum Field Theories. An introduction, written by the editors, provides an overview of the main underlying themes that bind together the papers in the volume.
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %strong Research monographs:
          %div{:style => "float:right;margin:0px 0px 0px 20px;"}
            %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/13422"}
              %img{:src => "/schreiber/files/CharacterMap-WSCover.jpg", :width => "300"}/
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/The+Character+Map+in+Non-Abelian+Cohomology"} The Character Map in Non-Abelian Cohomology
              %p
                (with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Domenico+Fiorenza"} D. Fiorenza
                \&amp;
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hisham+Sati"} H. Sati
              %p World Scientific (2023)
              %p
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/13422"}> doi:10.1142/13422
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %blockquote
            %p#Blurb
              %strong Cover blurb:
              This book presents a novel development of fundamental and fascinating aspects of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
              and
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+physics"} mathematical physics
              = succeed "\u201D" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead-generalized+cohomology"} extra-ordinary
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology+theories"} generalized cohomology theories
              enhanced to “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"}> twisted
              ” and
              = succeed "-geometric" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+homotopy+theory"} rational approximation
              by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Dold+character"} generalized Chern character
              maps and on the resulting
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charge+quantization"} charge quantization
              laws in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+theory"}
                higher
                = succeed "-form" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              appearing in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
              and in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+classification+of+topological+phases+of+matter"} classification of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+phase+of+matter"} topological
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+materials"} quantum materials
            %p
              Motivation for the conceptual re-development is the observation, laid out in the introductory chapter, that famous and famously elusive effects in strongly interacting (“
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+physics"}> non-perturbative
              ”)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} physics
              demand “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+cohomology"}> non-abelian
              ” generalization of much of established
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology+theory"} generalized cohomology theory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+cohomology"} non-abelian cohomology
              theory (”
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+infinity-bundle"}> higher gerbes
              “) has an esoteric reputation and has remained underdeveloped.
            %p
              The book’s theme is that variously
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology+theories"} generalized cohomology theories
              are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} best viewed
              through their
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+spaces"} classifying spaces
              – possibly but not necessarily
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite-loop+spaces"} infinite-loop spaces
              – from which perspective the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Dold+character"} character map
              is really an incarnation of the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+theorem+of+dg-algebraic+rational+homotopy+theory"} fundamental theorem of rational homotopy theory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+character"} Chern character
              and a multitude of scattered variants that have been proposed, but now seamlessly applying in the previously elusive generality of (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"}> twisted
              ,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential
              and)
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+cohomology"} non-abelian cohomology
            %p
              In laying out this result with plenty of examples, we provide modernized introduction and review of fundamental classical topics: 1. abstract
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
              via
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology"} generalized cohomology
              in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopical
              incarnation, 3.
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebraic
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+homotopy+theory"} rational homotopy theory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+theorem+of+dg-algebraic+rational+homotopy+theory"} fundamental theorem
              we re-cast as a (twisted) non-abelian
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+theorem"} de Rham theorem
              which naturally induces the (twisted) non-abelian character map.
          %p
            %br/
          %div{:style => "float:right;margin:-10px 0px 0px 20px;"}
            %a{:href => "/schreiber/show/Equivariant+Principal+infinity-Bundles"}
              %img{:src => "/schreiber/files/SS25Bun-CUP-Cover-251111.png", :width => "300"}/
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Equivariant+Principal+infinity-Bundles"}
                    Equivariant Principal
                    = succeed "-Bundles" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mn ∞
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              %p
                (with
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hisham+Sati"} H. Sati
              %p Cambridge University Press
              %p (2025, in press)
          %blockquote
            %p
              %strong In this book
              we prove classification results for stable
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+principal+bundle"}
                equivariant
                = succeed "-principal" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Γ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              in the case when the underlying
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ʃ
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \esh \, \Gamma
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+group"} topological
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure+group"} structure group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              is
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-truncated"} truncated
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
              vanish in and above some degree
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-gerbe"} higher
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+gerbes"} non-abelian gerbes
              and generally of equivariant
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+infinity-bundles"}
                  principal
                  = succeed "-bundles" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn ∞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure+group"} structure
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-group"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                \-group
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ʃ
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \esh  \Gamma
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+homotopy+groups"} equivariant homotopy groups
              of the respective classifying
              = succeed "-spaces" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+cohomology"} non-abelian
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+cohomology"} group cohomology
              of the equivariance group with coefficients in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ʃ
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \esh  \Gamma
            %p
              The result is proven in a conceptually transparent manner as a consequence of a
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+Oka+principle"} smooth Oka principle
              which becomes available after faithfully embedding traditional
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+differential+topology"} equivariant topology
              into the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Geometric+Orbifold+Cohomology"} singular-cohesive homotopy theory
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+homotopy+theory"} globally equivariant
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-stack"} higher stacks
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+group"} discrete
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariance+groups"} equivariance groups
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+action"} acting properly
              on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
              (“
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+equivariant+homotopy+theory"}> proper equivariance
              ”) with resolvable singularities whence we are equivalently describing principal bundles on
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/good+orbifolds"} good orbifolds
            %p
              In setting up this proof, we re-develop the theory of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+principal+bundles"} equivariant principal bundles
              from scratch by systematic use of
              = succeed "\u2018s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internalization
              %a{:href => "equivariant+bundle#NotionsOfEquivariantLocalTriviality"} equivariant local triviality conditions
              considered in the literature are automatically
              %em implied
              by regarding
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+principal+bundles"} equivariant principal bundles
              as
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-torsor"} principal bundles
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
              to the
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping+groupoid"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
